{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"This Month in AWS DevOps Agent: November 2026 | AWS Cloud Operations Blog","context":"### Measure and improve investigation quality\n\nThree launches help teams see how well AWS DevOps Agent performs and validate changes before relying on them.\n\nou can now replay a completed investigation after you change skills, instructions, or integrations, then compare the replay with the original by grader score, time to root cause, tools and skills used, and investigation timeline. Replays take no mutative actions and do not change the original. Because replays query your live environment, data available at the time of the original investigation might not be available to the replay. To learn more, see Replaying investigations.\n\nA Review is a quality assessment of a completed investigation. It returns a helpful or not helpful verdict and a pass, fail, or not applicable result for five dimensions: scope alignment, evidence grounding, completeness, environment impact, and accuracy. AWS DevOps Agent now runs Reviews automatically on a sample of completed investigations, when a linked ServiceNow or PagerDuty ticket is resolved, 7 days after an investigation completes, or both. Automatic Reviews are on by default with a 10 percent sampling rate, which you can set from 1 to 100 percent on the Grader agent’s Configuration tab. To learn more, see Reviewing investigations.\n\nThe Incident RCA and Incident mitigation agents each have a new Dashboard tab. The Summary view shows metrics such as investigations, the percentage that found a cause, and the median time to first cause for a single time window. The Trend view plots these numbers over spans from 7 days to 6 months. To learn more, see Managed agents","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/mt/this-month-in-aws-devops-agent-november-2026/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"Replaying investigations","context":"## Replay results\n\nThe **Replays** card on the **Review & replay** tab lists all replays of the investigation. The card shows each replay's name, status, creation time, and duration. Choose **View** to open a replay's investigation, or **Compare** to compare it with the original.\n\nThe comparison shows the two runs side by side:\n\n* **Root cause** – the root cause that each run found.\n* **Grader score** – the review grade and the number of criteria that each run passed, with the verdict for each criterion.\n* **Time to root cause** – how long each run took to reach its root cause.\n* **Tools used** – the tools that each run called.\n* **Skills used** – the skills that each run read.\n* **Investigation timeline** – the steps of each run. Choose **Show timelines** to see them.\n\nIf you replay an investigation more than once, you can pick any two runs of the same investigation to compare","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/devopsagent/latest/userguide/working-with-devops-agent-replaying-investigations.html"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"Scaling to Production-Grade Testing","context":"## Investigation at Scale with AWS DevOps Agent\n\nMoving from \"my first load test\" to \"production-grade performance engineering\" requires not just larger tests but also mature analysis practices. Manual investigation of test results becomes impractical as test frequency and complexity increase. DLT's native AWS DevOps Agent integration transforms post-test analysis from a labor-intensive, manual process into an automated, repeatable workflow.\n\n**Scaling investigation across environments:**\n\nRegister multiple Agent Spaces for different purposes. A dedicated Agent Space per environment (development, staging, production) allows each team to investigate independently without cross-contamination of findings:\n\n| Agent Space | Purpose | When to Investigate |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Dev Agent Space | Early regression detection | After every PR merge smoke test |\n| Staging Agent Space | Pre-release validation | After full load tests on staging |\n| Production Agent Space | Production incident correlation | After canary tests or observed degradation |\n\n**Building a historical investigation record:**\n\nEvery DevOps Agent investigation is stored in the DLT Investigations tab for the lifetime of the test run record. Over time, this builds a searchable knowledge base of performance findings. Use this history to:\n\n* **Track regression patterns.** If the same component appears as a bottleneck across multiple investigations, it signals a systemic architectural issue rather than a one-time configuration problem\n* **Validate remediation effectiveness.** After implementing a fix, compare the investigation findings from the next test run against the original. If the root cause no longer appears, the fix was effective\n* **Inform capacity planning.** Historical investigations reveal which components hit limits first under increasing load, helping you prioritize scaling investments\n\n**Integrating DevOps Agent into CI/CD pipelines:**\n\nCombine the DLT CLI with DevOps Agent for fully automated performance gate workflows:\n\n1. **CI/CD pipeline triggers a load test** via the DLT CLI after deployment to staging\n2. **DLT compares results against baseline** and detects if any SLO is violated\n3. **If a regression is detected**, the pipeline automatically triggers a DevOps Agent investigation\n4. **The investigation findings** are captured and attached to the deployment record\n5. **The pipeline fails the deployment** with actionable root-cause information, not just a \"latency exceeded threshold\" error\n\nThis pattern transforms performance gates from binary pass/fail signals into diagnostic workflows that tell engineers *why* performance degraded and *what to fix*, eliminating the manual triage step\n\n*[truncated — additional content omitted]*","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/solutions/latest/performance-testing/scaling-to-production-grade.html"}]}}

## Recent AWS announcements (What's New)

Newer than your training data may be. When these conflict with the documentation results above or with what you remember, prefer the most recent dated source.

{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"AWS DevOps Agent adds quality improvement dashboards and tools - AWS","context":"AWS DevOps Agent has introduced new quality improvement dashboards and tools to help users understand and enhance the performance of their instances. The new features include quality and latency dashboards that track results, investigation data on root cause identification and resolution time, and mitigation data on plan execution time and operator ratings. Trend views are also available to monitor improvements over time. A grader agent rates each investigation and compares its ratings with those of human operators to focus on discrepancies. Users can improve their instances by modifying skills, memories, and tools, and can test changes using a replay capability that lets them compare the results of the original and modified runs. The document also mentions the availability of the latest features on the release history page and the supported AWS Regions on the Regions table","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/11/aws-devops-agent-quality-dashboards/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"aws-well-architected-review","skill_description":"Performs a full AWS Well-Architected Framework review evaluating every framework question across all pillars discovered from the live AWS documentation by analyzing code, IaC, and configurations to produce evidence-backed findings with Eisenhower-prioritized remediation. Supports full reviews (every framework best practice with BP ID citations), quick reviews (question-level), pillar-scoped reviews, and lens-specific reviews using lenses discovered from the live AWS documentation. Also turns findings or named best practices into guardrails mapped to BP IDs (Config rules, CloudWatch alarms, permissions boundaries, SCPs, cfn-guard, CDK Aspects), deployed on user confirmation and verified. Triggers on mentions of Well-Architected review, WA review, WAR, WAFR, pillar assessment, architecture review, workload assessment, cloud readiness evaluation, or Well-Architected guardrails. Does not apply to learning WA concepts without assessing a workload, ADRs, or migration readiness assessments.\n\nServices: well-architected, config, cloudwatch, iam, organizations, cloudformation, cdk\nTasks: review, assess, audit, optimize, enforce\nPersona: solutions-architect, developer, platform-engineer, devops\nWorkload: general","skill_name":"aws-well-architected-review"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"aws-investigation","skill_description":"Investigates what is happening in an AWS account from evidence already recorded there. Not for capturing new data from running code (snapshots of function arguments on a live service, Dynamic Instrumentation), questions answered from the CloudWatch Omni context graph or Application Signals service map, or building or changing infrastructure. Covers reading deployment records instead of the newest stored version (completed AppConfig deployments, the version an alias points to, a stack's current template), following a request through every service's own logs (exact per-layer fields, unique failed requests in an alarm's breach window, failure boundary per event), and answering questions from the account's data (exact counts and breakdowns, coverage of the requested range, exact filters and joins, the rule behind failed or unmatched records, verbatim values). Use to triage an alarm, error spike or failed request, trace a request across services, or count, break down, extract or join records.\n\nServices: cloudwatch, appconfig, lambda, cloudformation, dynamodb, s3, athena, glue\nTasks: debug, investigate, query, troubleshoot\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: observability, operations","skill_name":"aws-investigation"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"aws-iam","skill_description":"Provides verified corrections for IAM behaviors that AI agents frequently get wrong — policy evaluation edge cases, trust policy gotchas, STS session limits, Organizations quirks, and SAML/MFA specifics. Also provides structured workflows for IAM role management and baseline policy generation from application source code or a Terraform plan JSON. Covers condition operator safety (ForAnyValue/ForAllValues with Null checks), bucket policy deny patterns (VPC endpoint restrictions, org paths), confused deputy protection, and service role creation for AWS services (Glue, CloudTrail, Lambda, ECS, etc.) with aws:SourceAccount/aws:SourceArn trust conditions. Applies when creating IAM roles, writing IAM or bucket policies, generating policies from application source code or a Terraform plan JSON, working with STS, Organizations, or condition operators, or any task needing a service or execution role. Does not cover non-IAM authorization like Cognito user-pool policies or app-level RBAC.\n\n\nServices: iam, sts, organizations\nTasks: deploy, configure, secure, policy-generation, permissions, source-code-to-policy, terraform-plan-to-policy\nPersona: developer, devops, architect, security-engineer\nWorkload: serverless, containers, data-analytics, security\n*","skill_name":"aws-iam"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Search docs on replay and investigation grading"}

